+# Mod/Resc Parsimony Inference — arXiv:1002.1292
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate | succinct exact Boolean matrix certificate |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: first and centered-third moments recover the affine factor maps |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+This generator turns [Nor et al., *Mod/Resc Parsimony Inference*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1002.1292) into an exact witness problem. The solver receives the paper's Boolean compatibility matrix (C) and must give a short affine specification of Boolean Mod and rescue matrices (M,R). Verification expands those matrices and recomputes every entry of the paper's asymmetric product. A skew-set fooling set supplies one incompatible cross per pair of gene coordinates, so the same exact check proves that the submitted width is minimal.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+Definitions 1–3 in Section 2 fix the asymmetric product and the minimum-width objective. Theorem 1 in Section 3 identifies it with biclique edge cover; Theorem 2 gives NP-completeness and inapproximability. Those worst-case results do **not** justify Track A for a planted distribution. Section 4 is the important easy regime: Rules 1–4 give an (O(N^3)) kernel and Theorem 4 gives an FPT algorithm in width (k), so keeping (k) small would be misleading.
+
+The generated affine subclass has an explicit polynomial reference algorithm: enumerate the (p(p-1)(p-2)) allowed triples per block and compare their products, (O(bp^5)) in the worst case. At the current shipping candidate (`easy`, (p=41,b=2)), one measured instance took 154,417 exact membership tests and 0.066 seconds; the eight-seed panel took 911,832 tests and 0.878 seconds. This is easy with code and not executable by hand. The compact route uses the three supplied moment residues to recover (c^{-1}), then (a,c,delta), in at most 48 modular operations. That mechanical-versus-compact gap is the Track B claim.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The `demo` preset uses (p=11), one block, and is genuinely hand-solvable with the displayed moment identity. Seed 3 renders the following complete instance data (the general definitions and output contract are the same as in `render`):
+
+```text
+p=11, k=11
+D=[0,1,2,4,5,8]
+sum_D=9, third_D=8, row0_sum=0, row1_sum=9, row0_third=1
+
+columns:
+0=(b0,y10) 1=(b0,y4) 2=(b0,y9) 3=(b0,y1) 4=(b0,y7) 5=(b0,y0)
+6=(b0,y5) 7=(b0,y6) 8=(b0,y8) 9=(b0,y2) 10=(b0,y3)
+
+rows: displayed-index (block,local-x) bitstring
+0  (b0,x2)  00101111010
+1  (b0,x0)  11000001111
+2  (b0,x1)  11100101010
+3  (b0,x6)  01010101110
+4  (b0,x10) 10011001101
+5  (b0,x7)  01101100110
+6  (b0,x5)  11010011100
+7  (b0,x9)  10011110001
+8  (b0,x3)  00111010011
+9  (b0,x8)  01101110001
+10 (b0,x4)  10110010101
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>{"blocks":[{"a":3,"c":2,"delta":7}]}</answer>`. `verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`; changing `delta` to 11 returns `(False, "block 0 field element out of range")`.
+
+## Difficulty
+
+| preset | prime (p) | blocks | matrix side | certificate atoms | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 11 | 1 | 11 | 3 | hand example; never ships |
+| easy | 41 | 2 | 82 | 6 | current shipping candidate |
+| medium | 71 | 2 | 142 | 6 | available escalation |
+| hard | 101 | 2 | 202 | 6 | available escalation |
+
+`n` is the field prime. Raising it enlarges the matrix and affine search space without lengthening the answer. `escalate` first adds a block, then raises the prime; it returns `cap_bound` before the rendered instance becomes unreasonable.
+
+## Local gates
+
+| gate | result | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 16/16 preset-seed instances; compact moment recovery also 16/16 |
+| G2 | pass | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and out-of-range corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged JSON round-trips through prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structured guesses; exact language size 4,090,881,600 |
+| G5 | pass | exactly 1 valid answer; density (2.4444608712\times10^{-10}); baseline 154,417 tests |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks each 0/8; reference enumerator 8/8 as Track B expects |
+| G7 | pass | doubled from (p=41) to supported prime (p=83), still verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 80/80 composed invariance checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 63 characters, 6 atoms, about 16 tokens, 48 intended operations |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+The required script was invoked, but OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` for every redraw. The harness correctly recorded these calls as errors and refused to make a hardness claim. They are not model failures and are not reported as such.
+
+| run | preset | usable attempts | solved | status |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0 | 0 | blocked by OpenRouter key limit |
+| structural hint | easy | 0 | 0 | pending usable key |
+| placebo hint | easy | 0 | 0 | pending usable key |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo diagnostic is therefore pending, not zero evidence. When a usable key is supplied, rerun the bare harness first, then each hint mode in its own scratch directory so the bare transcript is not overwritten.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_1002_1292 as g
+
+params = g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **params)
+text = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer('<answer>{"blocks":[{"a":1,"c":2,"delta":0},{"a":3,"c":4,"delta":5}]}</answer>')
+ok, reason = g.verify(inst, answer)
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit instances with `bash scripts/emit.sh 1002.1292 20` after a successful bare hardening transcript exists.
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is a deliberately structured affine subclass, not evidence that random Mod/Resc data are average-case hard. The exact G4 density is under the declared uniform prior on distinct nonzero affine multipliers; it says nothing about a solver using the moment prior. Indeed, the moment algorithm solves every generated instance efficiently—that is why this is Track B. The attack panel tests equal-degree/identity selection, a sorted-boundary greedy rule, 256 random restarts, and the obvious linear-moment ansatz; it does not test generic SAT/SMT or a production maximal-biclique package. The paper's much larger FPT bound is stated, not implemented.
+
+`canonical_key` normalizes each public difference set under affine maps and is invariant under row/column order, block names, and public affine coordinate labels. It is not a complete isomorphism test for arbitrary Cayley-tournament isomorphisms, so a rare non-affine duplicate could evade it. Finally, the required cross-vendor oracle and G9 evidence is still missing because the supplied OpenRouter key is over its total limit; this directory must not be submitted as hardened until those script-owned runs complete.
