+This is a native exact family based on [Nor et al., *Mod/Resc Parsimony Inference* (arXiv:1002.1292)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1002.1292). The solver receives the paper's Boolean compatibility matrix (C), with labelled rows and columns in independently shuffled presentation order, and must give affine triples defining Boolean Mod and rescue matrices (M,R). The checker expands the triples and recomputes every entry of the paper's asymmetric product (C=M\otimes R). It also constructs a fooling set of size (k) and checks its diagonal and cross-entries exactly, so the claimed width is certified minimal rather than merely feasible.
 
 ## Why this is Track B
 
-Definitions 1–3 in Section 2 fix the asymmetric product and the minimum-width objective. Theorem 1 in Section 3 identifies it with biclique edge cover; Theorem 2 gives NP-completeness and inapproximability. Those worst-case results do **not** justify Track A for a planted distribution. Section 4 is the important easy regime: Rules 1–4 give an (O(N^3)) kernel and Theorem 4 gives an FPT algorithm in width (k), so keeping (k) small would be misleading.
+Section 2, Definitions 1–3 fix the product. Section 3, Theorem 1 equates width-(k) factorizations with (k)-biclique edge covers, and Theorem 2 proves the unrestricted problem NP-complete. That does **not** establish hardness for this generated distribution. More importantly, Section 4 gives an (O(N^3)) kernel and Theorem 4 gives an FPT exact algorithm parameterized by (k); small fixed (k) therefore cannot support an honest Track A claim.
 
