-This generator turns [Nor et al., *Mod/Resc Parsimony Inference*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1002.1292) into an exact witness problem. The solver receives the paper's Boolean compatibility matrix (C) and must give a short affine specification of Boolean Mod and rescue matrices (M,R). Verification expands those matrices and recomputes every entry of the paper's asymmetric product. A skew-set fooling set supplies one incompatible cross per pair of gene coordinates, so the same exact check proves that the submitted width is minimal.
+This is a native exact family based on [Nor et al., *Mod/Resc Parsimony Inference* (arXiv:1002.1292)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1002.1292). The solver receives the paper's Boolean compatibility matrix (C), with labelled rows and columns in independently shuffled presentation order, and must give affine triples defining Boolean Mod and rescue matrices (M,R). The checker expands the triples and recomputes every entry of the paper's asymmetric product (C=M\otimes R). It also constructs a fooling set of size (k) and checks its diagonal and cross-entries exactly, so the claimed width is certified minimal rather than merely feasible.
 
 ## Why this is Track B
 
-Definitions 1–3 in Section 2 fix the asymmetric product and the minimum-width objective. Theorem 1 in Section 3 identifies it with biclique edge cover; Theorem 2 gives NP-completeness and inapproximability. Those worst-case results do **not** justify Track A for a planted distribution. Section 4 is the important easy regime: Rules 1–4 give an (O(N^3)) kernel and Theorem 4 gives an FPT algorithm in width (k), so keeping (k) small would be misleading.
+Section 2, Definitions 1–3 fix the product. Section 3, Theorem 1 equates width-(k) factorizations with (k)-biclique edge covers, and Theorem 2 proves the unrestricted problem NP-complete. That does **not** establish hardness for this generated distribution. More importantly, Section 4 gives an (O(N^3)) kernel and Theorem 4 gives an FPT exact algorithm parameterized by (k); small fixed (k) therefore cannot support an honest Track A claim.
 
-The generated affine subclass has an explicit polynomial reference algorithm: enumerate the `p*(p-1)*(p-2)` allowed triples per block and compare their products, with `O(blocks*p^5)` worst-case cost. At the current shipping candidate (`easy`, `p=41, blocks=2`), one measured instance took 154,417 exact membership tests and 0.100 seconds; the eight-seed panel took 911,832 tests and 0.349 seconds. This is easy with code and not executable by hand. The compact route uses the three supplied moment residues to recover `c^-1`, then `a,c,delta`, in at most 48 modular operations. That mechanical-versus-compact gap is the Track B claim.
+For this affine subclass an explicit mechanical algorithm exists: enumerate the (p(p-1)(p-2)) legal triples per block and compare their predicted matrix entries. Its worst-case cost is (O(bp^5)). At the shipping preset (p=41,b=2), the eight reference runs succeeded 8/8 and used 911,832 Boolean membership tests in 1.706585 seconds total; one separately measured shipping instance used 154,417 tests in 0.558137 seconds. The compact route instead uses the supplied first and centered-third moments. Because (p\equiv2\pmod3), cubing is bijective in (\mathbb F_p^*\); the cubic scale recovers (c), after which two linear moment differences recover (a) and (\delta). The implementation budgets at most 48 modular operations. Discovering and carrying out those identities unaided is the intended no-tool task.
 
