# Mod/Resc Parsimony Inference generator

| profile field | value |
|---|---|
| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
| Native domain | algebra |
| Object regime | finite field |
| Computational core | linear algebra |
| Certificate form | matrix certificate (a compact affine specification of the two Boolean matrices) |
| Intended intuition | invariant: first and centered-third moments recover hidden affine coordinate maps |
| Domain essentiality | native |
| Reduction | none |

This is a native exact family based on [Nor et al., *Mod/Resc Parsimony Inference* (arXiv:1002.1292)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1002.1292). The solver receives the paper's Boolean compatibility matrix (C), with labelled rows and columns in independently shuffled presentation order, and must give affine triples defining Boolean Mod and rescue matrices (M,R). The checker expands the triples and recomputes every entry of the paper's asymmetric product (C=M\otimes R). It also constructs a fooling set of size (k) and checks its diagonal and cross-entries exactly, so the claimed width is certified minimal rather than merely feasible.

## Why this is Track B

Section 2, Definitions 1–3 fix the product. Section 3, Theorem 1 equates width-(k) factorizations with (k)-biclique edge covers, and Theorem 2 proves the unrestricted problem NP-complete. That does **not** establish hardness for this generated distribution. More importantly, Section 4 gives an (O(N^3)) kernel and Theorem 4 gives an FPT exact algorithm parameterized by (k); small fixed (k) therefore cannot support an honest Track A claim.

For this affine subclass an explicit mechanical algorithm exists: enumerate the (p(p-1)(p-2)) legal triples per block and compare their predicted matrix entries. Its worst-case cost is (O(bp^5)). At the shipping preset (p=41,b=2), the eight reference runs succeeded 8/8 and used 911,832 Boolean membership tests in 1.706585 seconds total; one separately measured shipping instance used 154,417 tests in 0.558137 seconds. The compact route instead uses the supplied first and centered-third moments. Because (p\equiv2\pmod3), cubing is bijective in (\mathbb F_p^*\); the cubic scale recovers (c), after which two linear moment differences recover (a) and (\delta). The implementation budgets at most 48 modular operations. Discovering and carrying out those identities unaided is the intended no-tool task.

Each block's set (D) contains 0 and exactly one of (z,-z). This prevents one biclique from covering two entries in the checked fooling set. The generator also rejects (D) with nontrivial affine stabilizers, making the normalized affine certificate unique in the shipping measurements. The Section 4.1 easy cases are avoided by construction rather than hidden: the relevant affine blocks do not reveal the answer through degrees, and presentation order is shuffled.

## Worked demo (seed 0)

This is the complete rendered `demo` instance. A person can solve it on paper: it has one (11\times11) block and one three-residue answer, though the moment arithmetic still takes care.

```text
MOD/RESC PARSIMONY INFERENCE — AFFINE CERTIFICATE

All arithmetic on labels in this problem is modulo the prime p=11. There are
1 blocks and k=11 genes, labelled
(block,g) with g in 0,...,10. Rows represent Mod strains and columns
represent rescue strains. The displayed Boolean compatibility matrix C has
11 rows and 11 columns. Matrix rows and
columns are deliberately shown in unrelated orders; their block and local
labels are given below. Bit 1 means incompatible and bit 0 means compatible.

For Boolean length-k rows U,V define U tensor V to be 1 exactly when at least
one coordinate has U[g]=1 and V[g]=0; otherwise it is 0. Applied row-by-row,
this is the paper's Mod/Resc matrix product C=M tensor R.

Your certificate contains one affine triple (a,c,delta) for each block, in
block index order. Both a and c must lie in 1,...,10, they must be
different, and delta lies in 0,...,10. The triple defines full Boolean
factor matrices of common width k as follows.

* Mod row (block b,label x) has its only 1 at gene (b,a*x).
* Rescue row (block b,label y) has 0 at gene (b,g) exactly when
  c*y+delta-g modulo p belongs to that block's displayed set D. It has 1 at
  all other genes, including genes of other blocks.

The certificate is valid when these exact matrices satisfy every displayed
bit of C=M tensor R. It is then automatically parsimonious: each D contains 0
and exactly one of z and -z for every nonzero z. Pairing each gene g with Mod
row a^(-1)g and rescue column c^(-1)(g-delta) gives k 1-entries such that no
one factor coordinate can cover two of them. Thus k factors are necessary as
well as sufficient; this is an exactly checkable fooling-set lower bound.

For audit, each block also gives exact residues useful for checking structure.
sum_D is the sum of D. third_D is sum((d-mean_D)^3 for d in D), where
mean_D=sum_D/|D| in F_p. row0_sum and row1_sum are the sums of local column
labels y at 1-bits in local rows x=0 and x=1. row0_third is the analogous
centered third moment for row x=0. All six quantities are residues modulo p.

BLOCK DATA
Block 0: D=[0, 3, 4, 6, 9, 10]; sum_D=10; third_D=4; row0_sum=2; row1_sum=10; row0_third=10

DISPLAYED COLUMN ORDER
  0=(b0,y7)  1=(b0,y6)  2=(b0,y8)  3=(b0,y2)  4=(b0,y0)  5=(b0,y10)  6=(b0,y3)  7=(b0,y5)
  8=(b0,y9)  9=(b0,y1)  10=(b0,y4)

DISPLAYED MATRIX ROWS: displayed-index: (block,local-x) bitstring
  0: (b0,x10) 00001110111
  1: (b0,x4) 11010010011
  2: (b0,x8) 00011101011
  3: (b0,x6) 01011011010
  4: (b0,x0) 01100011101
  5: (b0,x1) 00111110100
  6: (b0,x7) 11101101000
  7: (b0,x2) 10110011001
  8: (b0,x3) 10110100110
  9: (b0,x9) 11000101101
  10: (b0,x5) 11101000110

Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one compact JSON object
with exactly the key "blocks" and entries {"a":int,"c":int,"delta":int}.
Example format: <answer>{"blocks":[{"a":1,"c":2,"delta":0}]}</answer>
Output nothing else inside the tags.
```

The planted answer is `<answer>{"blocks":[{"a":8,"c":6,"delta":7}]}</answer>`. `verify` returns `(True, "ok")`. Replacing `delta` by 11 returns `(False, "block 0 field element out of range")`.

